hadiths[3]{hadithnumber,text}:
"23038","Abdullah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us: Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us: al-Dahhak ibn Uthman narrated to us, on the authority of al-Maqburi, on the authority of Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Prophet of Allah, I ask you about what you know and I am ignorant of: of night and day, an hour in which prayer is disliked. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you pray dawn, refrain from prayer until the sun rises; when it has risen, pray, for prayer is attended and accepted until it stands overhead like a spear; when it stands overhead, that hour Hell is stoked and its gates opened until it passes your right eyebrow; when it passes your right eyebrow, pray, for prayer is attended and accepted until you pray Asr."
"23039","Abdullah narrated to us: Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali ibn Bahr ibn Kathir al-Saqqa narrated to us: Abu Qutaybah narrated to us: Umar ibn Nabhan narrated to us: Sallam Abu Isa narrated to us: Safwan ibn al-Muattal narrated to us, he said: We went out as pilgrims, and when we were at al-Arj, we saw a snake writhing, and it did not remain but died. A man took out a cloth from his bag, wrapped it in it, buried it, and dug for it in the ground. When we came to Makkah, while we were in the Sacred Mosque, a person stood over us and said: Which of you is the companion of Amr ibn Jabir? We said: We do not know him. He said: Which of you is the companion of the jann? They said: This one. He said: Indeed, may Allah reward you with good; indeed he was among the last of the nine to die who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) listening to the Quran."
"23040","Abdullah narrated to us: Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to me: Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed me, on the authority of Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, on the authority of Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, on the authority of Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and I observed his prayer one night: he prayed the last Isha, then slept; when it was midnight he woke, recited the last ten verses of Surah Al Imran, then used the toothstick, then performed wudu, then stood and prayed two rakahs; I do not know whether his standing, his bowing, or his prostration was longer. Then he left and slept, then woke, recited the verses, then used the toothstick, then performed wudu, then stood and prayed two rakahs; I do not know whether his standing, his bowing, or his prostration was longer. Then he left and slept, then woke and did that, and he continued doing as he did the first time until he prayed eleven rakahs. Abdullah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to me: al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, on the authority of Asid ibn Abi Asid, on the authority of Muadh ibn Abdullah ibn Khubayb, on the authority of his father, he said: Rain and darkness befell us, and we awaited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pray for us. He came out, took my hand, and said: Say. I was silent. He said: Say. I said: What shall I say? He said: Say: He is Allah, One, and the two Muawwidhat, when you enter evening and when you enter morning, three times; it will suffice you every day, twice."
